```javascript
const activeAndName = (data) => {
  const active = data.filter((item) => item.active);
  const name = active.length > 0 ? active[0].name : "";
  return { active, name };
};

// Example usage
const data = [
  { id: 1, active: true, name: "John" },
  { id: 2, active: false, name: "Jane" },
  { id: 3, active: true, name: "Bob" },
];

const result = activeAndName(data);
console.log(result); // { active: [1], name: "John" }
```

In this example, we're using a function `activeAndName` that takes an array of objects and returns an object with the keys "active" and "name".

The function first filters the array to only include objects with a "active" property, and then returns the first object with a "name" property.

The output is a JSON object with the keys "active" and "name".

